1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Campus Compass asks a simple question. Can students see
why a campus policy answer applies? We built a source
grounded helpdesk prototype for our prompt engineering
project at Bennett University. It connects a limited set
of questions to official evidence and makes uncertainty
visible.

2
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A student looking up a rule may find a notice from a
different cohort. A certificate request may also need
action from staff. Bennett already provides registrar
resources and a service desk. Our prototype helps
students find evidence and routes them to the official
channel when appropriate.

3
00:00:42,000 --> 00:01:03,000
We reviewed repository documentation for university PDF
assistants, a hybrid retrieval chatbot, and Rulebook AI.
These projects already demonstrate citations, refusals
and conflict handling. We focused our work on Bennett
specific scope and an inspectable evaluation. The
comparison concerns documented features.

4
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Here is the attendance example from our helpdesk. The
user sees a curated answer and the official source
citation. The historical warning asks the student to
verify present applicability. The evidence format is
designed to help a student check what the document
actually said.

5
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A hostel fee or refund can depend on details the
assistant does not have. The workflow asks for those
details and avoids citing unrelated documents. The final
version also links to the official service desk so
students can contact the university when they need an
authoritative answer.

6
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:06,000
In the prompt studio we expose the instructions and data
sent to the local Qwen model. The application supplies a
permitted status and fact identifiers. The model emits a
structured draft. The validator checks that the
selection matches the evidence, and the renderer keeps
source wording under application control.

7
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Version three produced valid JSON in all eight
development cases, but passed only one complete output
contract. A draft can select a real fact that answers an
entirely different question. The validator checks
relevance and scope as well as JSON syntax and fact
membership.

8
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Version four used a trusted gate and narrower evidence.
Two outputs copied a placeholder. Version five changed
the schema representation to a formal typed object. It
passed the contract in all eight development cases.
Forty eight actual responses across six versions are
included for reproduction.

9
00:02:48,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The original router missed three paraphrases even when
retrieval found the relevant fact. Targeted aliases
recover those cases, and the final build passes the
existing forty two routing checks. Twelve additional
internal questions test status and fact selection. These
checks are small and were authored within the project.

10
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The standalone app uses curated evidence. The prompt
studio supports replay and optional local inference. A
wider study needs independently written student queries
and policy owner review, especially for current rules
and exceptions. The final package includes the report,
source, prompt history and evaluation results.
